    </figure>
    <div class="tldr">
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Role</dt><dd>Solo: game design, C++, content scripts and the character pipeline</dd>
        <dt>Dates</dt><dd>Sept 29 to Oct 4, 2026 (26 commits)</dd>
        <dt>Engine</dt><dd>Unreal Engine 5.8</dd>
        <dt>Languages</dt><dd>C++, about 14,000 lines in the game and editor modules (776 of them tests). Python, about 4,000 lines of editor tooling.</dd>
        <dt>Status</dt><dd>Prototype v0.5. A Windows Shipping build packages. I played all four cities with mouse and keyboard on Sept 29; the newer learning loop (briefings, hints, debriefs) has so far been checked with scripted play-in-editor runs.</dd>
        <dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/HamoodiM/LinguaSpy">github.com/HamoodiM/LinguaSpy</a></dd>
        {/* TODO-USER: the LinguaSpy repo is private, so the link above and the source links below are dead for visitors. Push the 7 local commits, make the repo public, then delete this comment. */}
        <dt>Build</dt><dd class="todo">TODO-USER: upload the Windows build (for example to itch.io) and link it here, or delete this row.</dd>
      </dl>
      <div>
        <h2>Worth a look</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#grader">The sentence grader</a>: position-by-position matching with accent folding, and its tradeoff.</li>
          <li><a href="#leitner">The Leitner scheduler</a>, which also picks the exercise type. You can drive it below.</li>
          <li><a href="#save">The save version that never got saved</a>, a serialization bug and its regression test.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="tldr__links"><a href={RESUME}>Résumé (PDF)</a><a href={url('#linguaspy')}>The dossier on the home page</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </ProjectHead>

  <div class="longform">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What I built</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Four cities, each with its own map and language pack (French, Spanish, Japanese, Italian), and 6 missions: two in Paris, two in Madrid, one each in Tokyo and Rome.</li>
        <li>Conversations stored as <code>UConversationAsset</code> node graphs (NPC line, player challenge, player choice, listening check, end) and missions as <code>UMissionAsset</code> objective graphs (talk to, reach a location, collect). Both are <code>UPrimaryDataAsset</code>s, and their <code>IsDataValid</code> overrides report broken graphs in the editor. No language content lives in C++.</li>
        <li>A sentence grader, a Leitner spaced-repetition scheduler, a scaffold policy and a fluency grade, all written as pure functions so they can be tested without a world.</li>
        <li>A feedback ladder: the first wrong attempt gets a hint and costs 40% of the cover penalty, the second shows the model answer and costs the rest.</li>
        <li>A cover meter (<code>UActorComponent</code> with delegates). When it reaches zero the mission stops and you practise the phrases you got wrong.</li>
        <li>An adaptive handler: an S or A grade on a mission gives you less translation help next time, a C gives you more.</li>
        <li>A versioned save format (v3) with step-by-step migrations and async saving.</li>
        <li>Occlusion fade: every tick a sphere sweep runs from the camera to the player, and buildings in the way fade out: the component writes a fade value into each mesh's Custom Primitive Data, which the toon material turns into a dither. Ground and low props are skipped by comparing their bounds to the player's feet.</li>
        <li>Pedestrians that wander to random reachable navmesh points around a home location, and NPCs that turn to face you while you talk.</li>
        <li>A Slate editor tab for authoring and validating conversations.</li>
        <li>Python scripts that build every conversation, mission and city layout through the editor's Python API, plus offline Piper TTS voices keyed by a SHA-1 of the text and voice settings so only changed lines are regenerated. Only the French lines are voiced so far.</li>
        <li>A Blender Python script that builds 7 low-poly characters from primitives and binds each part rigidly to one bone of the UE mannequin skeleton, so the stock animations work. It also works around an FBX import issue that put a ×100 scale on the root bone.</li>
        <li>An assessment mode and a local JSONL learning log for a planned pilot study. The log is off by default and the game has no network code.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Click-to-move and the camera rig come from Unreal's top-down template. I moved the input into the game's own folders and added the occlusion fade on top.</p>

      <h2 id="grader">Grading a sentence word by word</h2>
      <p>You answer a local by arranging word tiles or, once you know a phrase well, by typing it. Each challenge stores one or more accepted answers as token lists. Before comparing, every token goes through <code>NormalizeToken</code>: punctuation is stripped from both ends (including «», ¿, ¡ and full-width Japanese punctuation), the text is lower-cased with <code>FText::ToLower</code> because <code>FString::ToLower</code> only folds ASCII and would leave "CAFÉ" as "cafÉ", and curly apostrophes become straight ones.</p>
      <p>Then the player's tokens are compared with each accepted answer, position by position:</p>
      <CodeFigure href={`${LS}Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Language/SentenceGrader.cpp#L107-L123`} file="Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Language/SentenceGrader.cpp" lines="107–123" code={grader} />
      {/* TODO-USER: the code links on this page work only after the LinguaSpy repo is public and the local commits are pushed. */}
      <p>The score is matched positions minus the length difference, so the winning answer is also the closest in length. That answer supplies the right/wrong mark for every tile and the index of the first wrong word, which the UI highlights.</p>
      <p>If nothing passes, the grader runs the comparison again with accents folded through a small Latin-1 and Latin Extended-A table. A match there means you knew the words and only missed an accent, so the game gives an accent hint instead of a plain "wrong". Typed input is split on whitespace including the full-width space U+3000, and Japanese uses unspaced tile chunks with an optional romaji reading on each tile.</p>
      <p>The tradeoff: this is exact matching per position, not edit distance. One missing word early in a typed sentence marks every later word wrong. With tiles that hurts less, since every word is on screen, but for typed answers I'd try an alignment over tokens next.</p>
      {/* TODO-USER: check that the "tradeoff" and "next" sentences above match your own view, and rewrite them in your words. */}

      <h2 id="leitner">Scheduling reviews with Leitner boxes</h2>
      <p>Every phrase you answer becomes a review item keyed <code>&lt;conversation path&gt;#&lt;node id&gt;#&lt;choice index&gt;</code>. Items live in boxes 0 to 5. A pass without help moves the item up one box. A pass after peeking at the translation, or with extra scaffolding, keeps the box and reschedules it. A fail drops it to box 1, due again in ten minutes. Times are stored as UTC ticks.</p>
      <CodeFigure href={`${LS}Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Language/SrsScheduler.cpp#L33-L65`} file="Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Language/SrsScheduler.cpp" lines="33–65" code={srs} />
      <p>Review sessions take due items first, most overdue first, then lowest box, then by key so the order is deterministic. Spare slots can be filled with the weakest items that aren't due yet.</p>
      <p>The box also decides what kind of exercise you get. A new phrase is assembled from the right tiles only; boxes 1 and 2 add distractor tiles; boxes 3 and 4 ask you to type the missing word; box 5 asks for the whole sentence. Each mistake earlier in the same session drops the level by one. The typing levels only apply when the language pack allows typed input and puts spaces between words (<code>bAllowTypedInput &amp;&amp; bUsesWordSpacing</code>), so Japanese stays on tiles.</p>
      <CodeFigure href={`${LS}Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Language/ScaffoldPolicy.cpp#L10-L33`} file="Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Language/ScaffoldPolicy.cpp" lines="10–33" code={scaffold} />
      <p>I used Leitner boxes over SM-2 because the box number can drive the exercise type directly and is easy to show to the player. FSRS is on the list of later ideas.</p>
      {/* TODO-USER: the sentence above is inferred from the code and docs. Replace it with your real reason, or delete it. */}
    </div>

    <div class="wrap wide ls-try">
      <h3>Drive the scheduler</h3>
      <LeitnerBoxes />
    </div>

    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 id="save">The save version that never got saved</h2>
      <p>The player profile is a <code>USaveGame</code> subclass with <code>CurrentVersion = 3</code> and a <code>Migrate()</code> function that upgrades one step at a time (<code>if (Version &lt; 2)</code>, then <code>if (Version &lt; 3)</code>). The v2 to v3 step, for example, gives each city a handler mode based on an old setting and marks finished missions as already briefed so returning players don't get surprise briefings.</p>
      <p>The bug: <code>Version</code> originally defaulted to <code>CurrentVersion</code>. Unreal's SaveGame serialization skips properties whose value equals the class default, so the version was never written to disk. On load the missing property took the class default, which is the current version, and old profiles looked up to date and skipped migration.</p>
      <p>The fix was to make the default 0 on purpose and stamp the real version explicitly when a profile is created and every time it's saved. A regression test went in with the same commit.</p>
      <CodeFigure href={`${LS}Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Save/LinguaSpySaveGame.h#L205-L211`} file="Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Save/LinguaSpySaveGame.h" lines="205–211" code={saveH} />
      <CodeFigure href={`${LS}Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Save/LinguaSpySaveSubsystem.cpp#L34-L41`} file="Source/Personal/LinguaSpy/Save/LinguaSpySaveSubsystem.cpp" lines="34–41" code={saveCpp} />
      <p>Saving is async (<code>AsyncSaveGameToSlot</code>), and a migration on load is logged with the old and new version numbers.</p>

      <h2>Conversation editor</h2>
      <p>The editor module adds a tab under Tools, LinguaSpy. It has an asset list with a filter and "New" and "Validate all" buttons, a list of nodes indented by their distance from the start node, and a details view for the selected node. Every edit runs inside an <code>FScopedTransaction</code>, so undo works. Edits made in the tab stamp a <code>LinguaSpy.HandEdited</code> metadata tag on the package, and the Python content scripts skip tagged assets instead of overwriting hand edits.</p>
      <CodeFigure href={`${LS}Source/PersonalEditor/Private/SConversationEditor.cpp#L653-L655`} file="Source/PersonalEditor/Private/SConversationEditor.cpp" lines="653–655" code={editor} />
      <p>I haven't done a visual pass on this tab yet, so expect rough layout.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap wide">
      <h2 class="shots__h">More from the game</h2>
      <div class="shots">
        {shots.map((s) => (
          <figure>
            <Image src={s.img} alt={s.alt} widths={[480, 800, 1200]} sizes="(min-width: 44rem) 36rem, 100vw" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>{s.cap}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Not done yet</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A hands-on playtest of the Phase 4 and 5 learning loop (Safe house, briefings, hints, intel checks, debriefs). Those were verified with a scripted test driver that teleports the player instead of using input.</li>
        <li>Spanish and Italian voices. Tokyo stays text-only because of the voice licence.</li>
        <li>Two more missions (Tokyo and Rome), mission replay and per-city music.</li>
        <li>Testing the packaged build on another PC and a review of the content by native speakers.</li>
        <li>The pilot study itself. I'm not claiming any learning results.</li>
      </ul>

